// Study Lab LSAT bank. All stimuli, passages and questions are original writing (none copied or adapted
// from LSAC PrepTests). IDs are "lsat" + index, so once published, only append new items to the end.
//
// Item fields:
//   d     difficulty 1-3 (uses the same level system as the other subjects)
//   t     type label, "LR · ..." (Logical Reasoning) or "RC · ..." (Reading Comprehension)
//   len   "short" (2-4 sentence stimulus), "medium" (5-8 sentences) or "long" (passage set)
//   stim  LR stimulus; "\n" separates speakers in a dialogue
//   p     RC passage key in LSAT_PASSAGES (questions sharing a passage form one set, in this order)
//   q     question stem
//   c     5 choices, CORRECT FIRST (authoring order); the app shows them in fixed A-E order
//   a     display letter of the correct answer; the wrong answers fill the other slots in authored order
//   trap  index into c of the most tempting wrong answer; te explains why it is wrong
//   e     why the correct answer is right
//   type  (drills only, lsat55+) "typed" | "explain" | "order" | "match", with the fields described in js/practice.js

export const LSAT_PASSAGES = {
  "contracts": {
    "title": "Fixing one-sided consumer contracts",
    "kind": "comparative",
    "a": [
      "Consumer contracts—the terms of service, rental agreements, and loan documents that most people sign without a second glance—are typically drafted by lawyers for lawyers. A single agreement may run to thousands of words, much of it devoted to remote contingencies. Their length and density are not incidental. A clause that limits a company’s liability or authorizes automatic renewal is far less likely to provoke objection when it appears on page eleven, phrased in terms few readers understand. Legislators should therefore require that every consumer contract open with a one-page summary, written in plain language and tested on ordinary readers, setting out the terms most likely to matter: the total cost, how to cancel, and what rights the consumer gives up. Such a requirement would not restrict what businesses may offer; it would simply ensure that consumers can see what they are agreeing to. Markets work only when buyers can compare products, and a buyer who cannot understand a contract cannot compare it with another. Requiring a short summary is a modest burden, since businesses already know which of their terms matter most. Clear summaries would let consumers reward companies that offer fair terms, and over time competition itself would push unfair terms out of the market."
    ],
    "b": [
      "For decades, the standard response to one-sided consumer contracts has been to demand better disclosure: shorter documents, larger type, summaries in everyday language. The appeal of this approach is obvious. It costs governments little, it preserves freedom of contract, and it treats consumers as capable adults. Unfortunately, the evidence that it changes behavior is thin. Studies of online purchasing consistently find that only a tiny fraction of users open the terms at all, and simplifying those terms barely raises that fraction. Those who do open the terms rarely spend long enough on them to absorb more than a few lines. The problem is not that contracts are hard to read but that reading them is not worth the effort for any single transaction; a shopper buying a phone case will not spend ten minutes studying its warranty, however clearly it is written. Disclosure mandates may even backfire, by allowing businesses to argue that consumers were fully informed of terms they had no practical chance to consider. Where particular terms are reliably harmful—hidden renewal fees, say, or waivers of the right to sue—the more effective remedy is to prohibit or limit those terms directly, rather than to explain them more clearly. Such rules protect every consumer, including the great majority who will never read a word of the contract."
    ],
    "area": "Law"
  },
  "commons": {
    "title": "Governing the commons",
    "kind": "single",
    "paras": [
      "For much of the twentieth century, economists and policymakers who considered shared natural resources—fisheries, pastures, groundwater basins, forests—tended to accept a bleak forecast. When many people draw on a resource that no one owns, the reasoning went, each user captures the full benefit of taking a little more while bearing only a fraction of the cost of depletion. The rational choice for every individual therefore leads to ruin for all. Garrett Hardin’s 1968 essay gave this logic a memorable name, the “tragedy of the commons,” and its policy implication seemed equally clear: a commons must either be divided into private property or placed under the control of a central government that could restrict access.",
      "The political scientist Elinor Ostrom did not dispute that overuse occurs; she had studied depleted fisheries and overdrawn aquifers herself. What she questioned was the assumption that users are helpless to prevent it without outside intervention. Beginning with groundwater disputes in Southern California and expanding to case studies of Swiss alpine meadows, Japanese village forests, and irrigation systems in Spain and the Philippines, Ostrom and her collaborators documented communities that had managed common resources sustainably, in some cases for centuries, without either privatization or central control. Elsewhere, she found, outside efforts to impose state management had sometimes made matters worse, displacing local rules that users had long relied on.",
      "These successes, Ostrom argued, were not accidents of culture or goodwill. They tended to share a set of institutional features: clearly defined boundaries specifying who may use the resource; rules suited to local conditions; arrangements allowing most users to take part in revising those rules; monitors who are accountable to the users; and sanctions that begin mildly and escalate for repeat violations. Where these features were absent, the tragedy Hardin described often unfolded just as predicted. Ostrom’s point was thus not that communities always succeed, but that the outcome depends on rules that people are capable of designing, and that analysts who assume otherwise may recommend remedies that destroy working arrangements.",
      "Critics have noted that most of Ostrom’s cases involve relatively small, stable groups whose members interact repeatedly and can observe one another’s behavior—conditions that may not hold for resources such as the global atmosphere or the open ocean. Ostrom acknowledged this limitation, yet she maintained that even large-scale problems could benefit from what she called polycentric governance, in which many overlapping authorities—local, regional, and national—each address part of the problem. Her work, recognized with the Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences in 2009, did not overturn the insight that shared resources are vulnerable. Rather, it replaced a single prescription with a more demanding question: under what conditions can the people who depend on a resource govern it themselves?"
    ],
    "area": "Social science"
  },
  "ulcers": {
    "title": "Bacteria and the cause of ulcers",
    "area": "Science",
    "kind": "single",
    "paras": [
      "Until the 1980s, physicians were nearly unanimous about the cause of peptic ulcers, the painful sores that form in the lining of the stomach and upper small intestine. Ulcers, they held, resulted from an excess of stomach acid, often brought on by stress, rich or spicy food, or a hurried way of life. The stomach was thought to be too acidic for bacteria to survive in it for long, so an infectious cause was rarely considered. Treatment followed from the theory: patients were told to rest and change their diets, and they were prescribed drugs that reduced acid production. These drugs relieved symptoms, but once patients stopped taking them, the ulcers usually returned. For the most stubborn cases, surgeons removed part of the stomach.",
      "In 1979 the pathologist Robin Warren, working in Perth, Australia, noticed curved bacteria in tissue samples from inflamed stomachs. Spiral-shaped bacteria had occasionally been reported in human stomachs before, but they had generally been dismissed as contaminants or curiosities. With the young physician Barry Marshall, he found that the organism, later named Helicobacter pylori, was present in nearly all of their patients with ulcers. Their early attempts to grow it in the laboratory failed until a set of culture plates happened to be left in the incubator over a long holiday weekend; the slow-growing bacteria needed more time than the usual two days. Even so, a correlation between bacteria and ulcers could not by itself show which caused which, and many specialists suspected that the bacteria merely colonized tissue that was already damaged.",
      "Lacking a suitable animal model, Marshall took the unusual step in 1984 of drinking a culture of the bacteria himself. Within days he developed gastritis, the inflammation that can precede an ulcer, and biopsies confirmed that H. pylori had taken hold; a course of antibiotics then cleared it. More persuasive to the wider profession, however, were clinical trials in which patients given antibiotics, and thereby cleared of the infection, saw their ulcers heal and, unlike patients given acid-reducing drugs alone, rarely suffered a relapse.",
      "Acceptance came slowly. Many gastroenterologists remained skeptical through the late 1980s, and only in 1994 did a consensus panel of the U.S. National Institutes of Health recommend antibiotics for ulcer patients infected with H. pylori. Warren and Marshall received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 2005. Yet the older view was not simply wrong. Acid is still needed for an ulcer to form, a substantial share of ulcers are caused by common anti-inflammatory painkillers rather than by infection, and most people who carry H. pylori never develop an ulcer at all. What changed was the understanding of what usually turns a vulnerable stomach into an ulcerated one. Testing for the infection is now a routine part of diagnosing an ulcer."
    ]
  },
  "homer": {
    "title": "Formula and art in Homer",
    "area": "Humanities",
    "kind": "single",
    "paras": [
      "Readers of the Iliad and the Odyssey have long noticed how often the same phrases recur. Dawn is “rosy-fingered” again and again, Achilles is “swift-footed” even when he is sitting still, and whole lines describing a meal or the launching of a ship are repeated almost word for word. For centuries, scholars who assumed that Homer composed with pen in hand treated these repetitions as a puzzle, or as evidence that later editors had stitched together the work of several poets.",
      "In the late 1920s the American scholar Milman Parry proposed a different explanation. Studying the descriptive epithets attached to the names of gods and heroes, he showed that they formed a remarkably economical system: for a given character, in a given grammatical form, there was usually just one epithet that would fill a particular part of the hexameter line. Which epithet appeared depended less on the scene than on the space left in the line. Such a system, Parry argued, could not be the invention of one writer seeking variety; it was the accumulated craft of generations of oral poets, who needed ready-made phrases in order to compose verse aloud at the speed of performance. To test the idea, Parry and his assistant Albert Lord recorded singers of heroic songs in Yugoslavia in the 1930s. These singers, many of whom could not read, performed songs thousands of lines long, recomposing them with each performance from a stock of traditional phrases and scenes.",
      "Parry died in 1935, but Lord carried the work forward, and by the time his book The Singer of Tales appeared in 1960, the view that the Homeric poems emerged from an oral tradition had become widely accepted. Lord also argued that learning to read and write tended to erode a singer’s ability to compose in this way. Its success, however, created a new difficulty. If nearly every phrase was traditional, what remained of the poet? Some of Parry’s followers concluded that it made little sense to praise a particular word choice, since the poet had simply used whatever formula the meter required.",
      "Later critics have resisted that conclusion. A formula, they point out, is a resource rather than a cage: the tradition offered alternatives, and the poet chose when to use a stock phrase, when to depart from it, and how to arrange scenes for effect. When a hero’s familiar epithet of praise appears at the moment of his humiliation, the contrast between the ready-made phrase and the situation can sharpen the irony rather than dull it. Repetition, too, can be purposeful: a scene repeated with one small change draws attention to the change. On this view, the discovery of the oral tradition does not diminish the poet’s art. It changes the standard by which that art should be judged, from originality of phrase to mastery of a shared language."
    ]
  },
  "restorative": {
    "title": "Restorative justice and its critics",
    "paras": [
      "Conventional criminal justice asks three questions: what law was broken, who broke it, and what punishment the offender deserves. Restorative justice, an approach that has spread through youth justice systems in New Zealand, Canada and elsewhere in recent decades, asks different ones: who was harmed, what do they need, and whose obligation is it to meet those needs? In a typical restorative conference, the offender, the person harmed, their supporters and a trained facilitator meet to discuss what happened and agree on how the offender will make amends, often through an apology, repayment or community work. Offenders usually take part only after accepting responsibility for the harm they caused.",
      "Supporters point to studies finding that victims who take part report less fear and greater satisfaction than those whose cases go through court, and that some offenders reoffend less often afterward. Critics respond that participants choose to take part, so the people who enter restorative programs may differ from those who do not; offenders willing to face their victims might be less likely to reoffend in any case. Critics also worry that the pressure to reconcile can fall unfairly on victims. A victim who declines to meet the offender may feel that she has let the process down, and an offender who apologizes because it is expected may say little that is sincere.",
      "These objections are serious, but they argue for careful design rather than abandonment. Studies that assign eligible cases to a conference or to court by lot can separate the effect of the program from the effect of who volunteers, and several such trials have found modest but real benefits. In some of them, the largest benefits appeared for violent offences, the very cases in which many observers had expected the approach to work least well. Programs can also make participation genuinely optional for victims, with a conference going ahead only if the victim wishes.",
      "Nor should restorative justice be judged by reoffending alone. A court that imposes a sentence has done its job even if the person harmed leaves no less afraid and no better informed about why the offence happened. A conference that produces an honest account and a concrete plan for repair may be worthwhile on those grounds alone. The right comparison, then, is not between restorative justice and a perfect system, but between it and the system it would partly replace, judged by everything that system is supposed to accomplish."
    ],
    "area": "Criminology"
  },
  // ---- Study lessons (also listed in js/study.js) ----
  "juries": { title: "Plain-Language Jury Instructions", kind: "single", area: "Law and policy", paras: [
    "For generations, the instructions that judges read to jurors before deliberation were shaped chiefly by appellate courts. Because a conviction can be overturned if a trial judge misstates the law, the safest course for a judge was to repeat language that higher courts had already approved, however archaic. The result was a body of instructions that was legally impeccable yet frequently baffling to the people who actually had to apply it. Terms such as \"preponderance,\" \"proximate cause,\" and \"reasonable doubt\" were explained, if at all, with phrases nearly as opaque as the terms themselves. Appellate judges, for their part, reviewed the instructions as lawyers would, asking whether each sentence stated the law correctly rather than whether an ordinary listener could follow it.",
    "Reformers in many jurisdictions have responded by rewriting instructions in plain language: shorter sentences, familiar words, active verbs, and concrete examples. Their case rests on a simple premise. A jury that misunderstands the law cannot faithfully apply it, and a verdict reached through misunderstanding is legitimate only in form. Studies in which mock jurors are questioned after hearing instructions have repeatedly found that comprehension improves when the instructions are simplified.",
    "Critics raise two objections. First, they argue that legal terms carry precise meanings accumulated over centuries of judicial interpretation, and that paraphrasing them risks subtly altering the law. Second, they warn that each newly drafted instruction invites fresh appeals, since no higher court has yet approved its wording.",
    "Neither objection justifies the status quo. The first assumes that the precision of a legal term survives transmission to a listener who does not understand it, an assumption the comprehension studies undercut. Precision that exists only on the page is of little use in the jury room. The second objection is real but temporary: once a plain-language instruction has been reviewed and upheld, it enjoys the same protection as its predecessor.",
    "Still, reformers sometimes overstate what plain language can achieve. Simplifying vocabulary does nothing to fix instructions that are poorly organized, that are delivered orally in a single long sitting, or that ask jurors to perform reasoning tasks people find difficult regardless of wording, such as considering a piece of evidence for one purpose while ignoring it for another. The most promising reforms therefore treat clear language as a starting point rather than a destination. Giving jurors written copies, explaining the law before the evidence is heard as well as after, and testing draft instructions on members of the public before adopting them all address problems that rewording alone cannot reach.",
  ], terms: [["Appellate court", "A higher court that reviews the decisions of trial courts."], ["Mock jurors", "Volunteers who play the role of jurors in research studies."]] },
  "treerings": { title: "The Limits of Tree-Ring Dating", kind: "single", area: "Archaeology", paras: [
    "Dendrochronology, the dating of wood by the pattern of its annual growth rings, is often celebrated as the most precise dating method available to archaeologists and architectural historians. In temperate climates, a tree typically adds one ring each growing season, and the width of that ring reflects local conditions: wide in favourable years, narrow in harsh ones. Because trees in the same region respond to the same weather, they share a distinctive sequence of wide and narrow rings. By matching overlapping sequences from living trees, old buildings, and preserved timbers, researchers have assembled master chronologies reaching back thousands of years in some regions. A timber whose ring pattern fits such a chronology can be assigned to exact calendar years. The method depends on having enough rings to match with confidence: a short sequence may fit a chronology at several points, or at none.",
    "This precision, however, attaches to a narrower question than the one historians usually want answered. A ring sequence dates the growth of the tree and, if the outermost ring beneath the bark survives, the year the tree was felled. It does not, by itself, date the building or object made from it. The step from felling to construction requires assumptions the method cannot verify.",
    "The first difficulty is that the outer rings are often missing. The sapwood, the pale, younger wood just under the bark, was frequently trimmed away by carpenters or has since decayed. When it is absent, the last surviving ring provides only an earliest possible felling date, and the true date may be decades later. Researchers estimate the number of missing rings statistically, but such estimates yield a range, not a year.",
    "The second difficulty lies in human behaviour. Builders sometimes stockpiled timber, used it years after felling, or salvaged beams from older structures. A roof containing reused timbers will appear older than it is, and a single anomalous beam may reflect a later repair rather than the original construction.",
    "None of this diminishes dendrochronology's value; rather, it locates that value correctly. The strongest conclusions come from sampling many timbers in a structure, preferably those with the bark edge intact, and from reading the results alongside documentary records and architectural features. When several beams converge on the same felling season, the inference that construction followed soon afterward becomes compelling, because in many building traditions carpenters commonly worked timber green, that is, soon after cutting, before it dried. A single precise number, by contrast, invites a confidence the evidence does not always warrant.",
  ], terms: [["Dendrochronology", "The science of dating wood by matching patterns of annual tree rings."], ["Sapwood", "The younger, outer wood of a tree, just beneath the bark."], ["Master chronology", "A long, continuous record of ring patterns for a region, built by overlapping samples."]] },
};

